Apple yesterday <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/13/apple-homepod-mini-2/">announced the HomePod mini 2</a>, introducing the compact smart speaker's first major hardware update in six years.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/HomePod-minis-on-gradient-feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1067302" /><br/>
The new model retains the original's spherical design, but adds an S10 chip, faster wireless connectivity, Sound Glow lighting, new Mac and home theater capabilities, and support for <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI in a future software update.<br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/homepod-mini/">HomePod mini 2</a> costs &#36;149 in the United States, &#36;20 more than its predecessor's last Apple price of &#36;129. While the original launched for &#36;99 in 2020, Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/25/apple-tv-and-homepod-price-increases/">raised its price</a> in June 2026. With many core features carried over, is the new model worth upgrading to, and should you consider a discounted original instead?<br/>
<br/>
Both generations feature a full-range driver, two passive radiators, and an acoustic waveguide for 360-degree sound. They also share temperature and humidity sensors, Thread networking, Ultra Wideband proximity features, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/airplay/">AirPlay</a> multiroom audio, stereo pairing, Intercom, smart home control, and Sound Recognition. Their dimensions remain identical at 3.3 inches tall and 3.9 inches wide, and both include a 20W power adapter.<br/>
